    "html": "<h2>Batch 91 Forensic Discoveries</h2>\n<p>Batch 91 recovers and dates 14 historical recordings spanning college football lore, peak golden age domestic comedy, beloved canine adventure serials, jazz history retrospective analysis, symphonic Christmas celebrations, orchestral pop arrangements, and depression-era dance remotes: the legendary 1930 locker room recording of Notre Dame head coach Knute Rockne giving his immortal &#39;Inside and Outside&#39; pep talk on <em><strong>Great Moments in Sports</strong></em>, 3 classic comedy broadcasts of Fanny Brice and Hanley Stafford on <em><strong>The Baby Snooks Show</strong></em> (CBS / General Foods, early 1946), 2 broadcasts of the original radio drama <em><strong>Lassie</strong></em> (ABC / Red Heart Dog Food, 1947), Dr. Billy Taylor and Hal Jackson analyzing Duke Ellington&#39;s suite compositions on <em><strong>The Genius of Duke</strong></em> (1968), baritone Nelson Eddy singing &#39;Silent Night&#39; and the &#39;Hallelujah Chorus&#39; with Robert Armbruster&#39;s concert orchestra (1945), David Rose&#39;s lush strings playing &#39;The Continental&#39; (1949), Chick Martin&#39;s rural music on <em><strong>Checkerboard Time</strong></em> (1941), Anson Weeks from the Mark Hopkins Hotel (1933), Durward Kirby on <em><strong>Crisco&#39;s Radio Newspaper</strong></em> (1944), and a dramatic civil rights biography of NY Governor Al Smith on <em><strong>Lest We Forget</strong></em> (1948).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>Knute Rockne Pep Talk</em> &amp; <em>The Baby Snooks Show</em> (1930–1946)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Verified Airdate</th>\n<th>Historical &amp; Comedy Context</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Knute_Rockne_Pep_Talk.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Great Moments in Sports: Knute Rockne Delivers His Famous &#39;Inside and Outside&#39; Locker Room Pep Talk&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1930-11-08</strong></td>\n<td>Authentic 1930 locker room audio of Rockne urging the Fighting Irish to unselfish teamwork.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Baby_Snooks_Report_Card.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Baby Snooks Show: &#39;Snooks Gets Her Report Card&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1946-01-27</strong></td>\n<td>Fanny Brice and Hanley Stafford as Daddy Higgins dealing with Snooks&#39; school marks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Baby_Snooks_Robot.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Baby Snooks Show: &#39;Daddy and the Anatomy of a Robot&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1946-02-03</strong></td>\n<td>Daddy Higgins attempting to build a mechanical automaton while Snooks creates havoc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Baby_Snooks_Beach.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Baby Snooks Show: &#39;Daddy and Snooks at the Beach&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1946-02-10</strong></td>\n<td>Vacation misadventures by the seashore with the Higgins family.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Lassie</em> &amp; <em>The Genius of Duke</em> (1947–1968)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Verified Airdate</th>\n<th>Performers &amp; Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Lassie_One_Eyed_Cat.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Lassie: &#39;The One-Eyed Cat &amp; Forest Adventure&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1947-10-18</strong></td>\n<td>Lassie barking cues and rescuing wildlife in rural forest settings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Lassie_Dog_Named_Tramp.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Lassie: &#39;A Dog Named Tramp &amp; The Farm Boy&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1947-10-25</strong></td>\n<td>Lassie helping a stray canine find shelter and protecting farm livestock.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Duke_Ellington_Red_Carpet.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Genius of Duke: Billy Taylor &amp; Hal Jackson &#39;Red Carpet&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1968-05-15</strong></td>\n<td>Jazz pianist Billy Taylor analyzing Ellington and Billy Strayhorn&#39;s arrangement genius.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>3. <em>Nelson Eddy</em>, <em>David Rose</em>, <em>Checkerboard Time</em>, <em>Anson Weeks</em>, <em>Crisco</em>, &amp; <em>Al Smith</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Verified Airdate</th>\n<th>Broadcast Details</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Nelson_Eddy_Silent_Night.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Nelson Eddy Show: &#39;Silent Night&#39; with Robert Armbruster Orchestra&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1945-12-23</strong></td>\n<td>Classical baritone holiday special with full chorus and orchestra.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Nelson_Eddy_Hallelujah.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Nelson Eddy Show: &#39;Hallelujah Chorus&#39; from Handel&#39;s Messiah&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1945-12-30</strong></td>\n<td>Choral and orchestral year-end sacred concert performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>David_Rose_Continental.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Music of David Rose: &#39;The Continental&#39; &amp; Lush Strings&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1949-04-12</strong></td>\n<td>David Rose leading his distinctively orchestrated string ensemble.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Checkerboard_Darbys_Ram.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Checkerboard Time: &#39;Darby&#39;s Ram &amp; Rural Harmony&#39; (Chick Martin)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1941-09-18</strong></td>\n<td>Early morning country standard program for Purina Mills.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Anson_Weeks_Sweetheart.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Anson Weeks and His Orchestra: &#39;If I Could Call You Sweetheart&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1933-04-20</strong></td>\n<td>West coast hotel ballroom remote featuring smooth dance arrangements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Crisco_Radio_Newspaper.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Crisco&#39;s Radio Newspaper: Bernadine Flynn &amp; Durward Kirby&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1944-03-24</strong></td>\n<td>Daytime news and homemaking features presented by Durward Kirby.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Lest_We_Forget_Al_Smith.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Lest We Forget: &#39;The Story of Al Smith - The Happy Warrior&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1948-10-14</strong></td>\n<td>Institute for Democratic Education dramatization of Al Smith&#39;s life and tolerance.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n",
